tinycc-os-kernel: the self-hosting image
=========================================

This system was compiled, assembled and linked by the Tiny C Compiler (tcc) and the
project's own linker (tld): the kernel, busybox, musl, tcc itself.  This image also
carries what is needed to do that again, in here, without gcc, binutils, flex or bison:

  /usr/src/linux-*.tar.gz    the kernel source pruned to what this configuration builds
                             (every file the host build read, all Makefiles and Kconfig
                             files, Kbuild's scripts, the headers, arch/x86), with the
                             project's kernel patches applied
  /usr/src/tinycc.tar.gz     tcc with the project's patches
  /usr/src/tld.tar.gz        the linker suite (ld, nm, objcopy, readelf, ar, strip)
  /usr/src/make-*.tar.gz     GNU make
  /usr/bin/tcc, i386-tcc     the compiler (static, musl) and the i386 cross compiler that
                             tcc -m16 runs for the kernel's real-mode code
  /usr/bin/tld (ld nm objcopy readelf ar strip), /usr/bin/make
  /usr/bin/tcc-kcc, tcc-hostcc, objtool-stub    the Kbuild CC / HOSTCC / OBJTOOL wrappers
  /usr/bin/flex, bison       copy the pre-generated Kconfig lexer and parser from
                             /usr/share/tinycc-os-kernel/kconfig
  /usr/share/tinycc-os-kernel/{minimal,tcc}.config    the Kconfig fragments
  /usr/share/tinycc-os-kernel/patches                 the tcc and kernel patch series

Commands:

  build-kernel          unpack the kernel tree to /build (a tmpfs), configure it like the
                        host did (tinyconfig + the fragments) and run the same make line;
                        the result is /build/linux/arch/x86/boot/bzImage.  Its uname -v
                        counts the generation: #1 was built on the host, #2 in here, ...
  build-toolchain       rebuild tcc + i386-tcc and tld from the sources into /usr/local;
                        build-kernel then uses them (--make also rebuilds GNU make)
  send-file FILE...     copy files to the host over the second serial port (ttyS1); with
                        ./run.sh --selfhost the host gets them in out/x86_64/selfhost/
                        (scripts/recv-file.sh cuts them out of the capture)

The host boots the result with KERNEL=out/x86_64/selfhost/bzImage ./run.sh and the banner
shows generation #2.  Everything happens in RAM: ./run.sh --selfhost gives the machine 1 GB.
